# Subsystem architecture

## Responsibility and owner

*State the subsystem's responsibility and the owner of its authoritative state. Where relevant, separate game logic, presentation and view-local state using `docs/game-organization.md`; one business subsystem may contain both logic and presentation.*

*Give its main location and useful scene, script or resource entry points, or link a module README when that is enough. Link relevant verification entries when useful; test details stay in their own records. Keep this navigation even when the optional runtime-flow section is omitted.*

## Boundaries and allowed dependencies

*Name the key direct collaborators and what the subsystem uses each one for. Keep these separate from the permitted dependency directions. Explain why the boundaries exist and list any approved exceptions. Link back to `../architecture.md`.*

*Summarize the reasons needed to understand the current subsystem. Link local implementation rationale to its Feature design and cross-Feature rationale that meets the ADR conditions to its ADR. Earlier decisions stay in their own records; do not invent missing rationale or create an ADR just to fill this map.*

## Key runtime flows (optional)

*Describe flows whose ordering, ownership or failure behavior matters across modules. Trace the trigger, participating modules, authoritative state change and observable result, with links to the implementation. Use a short sequence or diagram only when it helps.*

*Include the details that affect this subsystem:*

- *Where Node, Resource, SceneTree or file I/O enters the flow, and where domain logic stays independent of the engine.*
- *How requests, accepted or rejected results, current state and one-time feedback connect. Link the requirements that define them. Explain animation timing or interruption conditions when they affect whether a game rule applies.*
- *Who creates and owns runtime state, resources and subscriptions, and who releases or cancels them on completion, scene exit or interruption.*
- *Where failure is detected and handled, what remains valid, and how failure or recovery reaches the caller or the presentation.*

*Mark which behavior has been observed and which is proposed or unverified. Inspect the actual call path; dynamic signals and loading may need runtime verification. Link interface definitions, implementation plans and tests where they already live. Omit this section when the local code already makes the flow clear.*

## Standalone technical decisions

*Use this section only for adopted decisions that have no Feature, do not meet the ADR conditions, and belong to this one subsystem. Follow **Standalone technical decisions** in `methodology/10-architecture-overview.md`, including its scope, authorization and migration rules. Keep the decision rationale separate from implemented facts. Omit this section when there is no such decision.*

## Extracted reference facts

*Link optional generated facts with the extractor, snapshot and scope. They do not replace the responsibility and dependency description above.*

## Maintenance

*Follow the **Navigation** and **Maintaining a map** sections of `methodology/10-architecture-overview.md` for content, change triggers and checks.*
